# Whitelist known-safe operator tooling that has "password" in the filename but contains
# no credential material. These scripts READ credentials at run time from Secrets Manager;
# they never carry credential values in their source. Added 2026-08-07 after the
# demo-identity-model spec's security-reviewer Cycle 4 caught that the *password* pattern
# was silently blocking both files from `git add`.
!deployment/scripts/write_demo_passwords.py
!deployment/scripts/test_write_demo_passwords.py

# Same class, found 2026-09-06 while adding username/password sign-in to the Connected
# Services portal. This is a vitest suite for auth.ts `signInWithPassword()`; every
# credential in it is a literal test fixture ("pw", "correct-password", "wrong-password")
# and the Cognito SDK is mocked, so no real credential material is present. Without this
# line the whole suite is invisible to `git add` and to CI — `git add` on a directory
# skips ignored files silently, with no error, so the omission is not observable.
!modules/connected_services_ui/src/__tests__/signInWithPassword.test.ts

# Whitelist an issue directory whose SLUG contains "secret" and is therefore caught by
# the `*secret*` pattern above. The pattern matches the DIRECTORY name, and git does not
# descend into an excluded directory, so the directory itself must be negated — negating
# only the files inside it would not work.
#
# Contents verified credential-free before whitelisting (2026-08-10): the archived Lambda
# log carries only resource names, usernames, request ids, a Secrets Manager *VersionId*
# (a UUID, not a value) and an S3 host-id. The setter handler redacts its exception
# detail, which is why the log is safe — and also why root cause had to be established
# from the synthesised template instead. Same class of false positive as the two scripts
# above: the word "secret" describes the subject; it is not a secret.
#
# Precedent: issues/2026-05-27-federate-oauth-secret-rotation-deferred/ is tracked for
# the same reason.
!issues/2026-08-10-cms-persona-secret-arn-suffix-iam-mismatch/

# Account-specific and sensitive content - DO NOT COMMIT
#
# NOTE (2026-08-04): filename patterns naming specific AWS account IDs were REMOVED
# from this file. They ignored files whose *names* contained our account IDs, but
# `.gitignore` itself ships to the public mirror and is listed in `scan_exclude`, so
# the patterns published the very identifiers they existed to protect — and the
# scanner never inspected the file. See
# `issues/2026-08-04-account-id-published-via-gitignore/`.
#
# A denylist written in terms of the secret embeds the secret. Account-specific
# ignore rules belong in a per-clone location that is never published:
#
#     printf '*<your-account-id>*\n*arn:aws:iam::<your-account-id>*\n' >> .git/info/exclude
#
# `.git/info/exclude` has identical semantics to `.gitignore` and is not version
# controlled, so it is per-developer. Content-level protection is unaffected: the
# pre-publish scanner matches these IDs inside file contents via
# `.publish-secrets-scan.yml`, which is the defence that actually mattered here.
*bootstrap*.amazonaws.com*
*arn:aws:secretsmanager*

